592186064857 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534654 17592186045417 true] [17592186064857 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}({cot}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139534654 17592186045417 true] [17592186064857 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{1}{\\cos^{2} x}(\\cot^{2} x + \\cos^{2} x\\sin x)dx$?" 13194139534654 17592186045417 false] [17592186064857 1074 "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity ${cot}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}({cot}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {cot}^{2}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({csc}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534654 17592186045417 true] [17592186065085 1061 1675 13194139534654 17592186045417 false] [17592186065085 1061 639749 13194139534654 17592186045417 true] [17592186065086 1061 1676 13194139534654 17592186045417 false] [17592186065086 1061 639750 13194139534654 17592186045417 true] [17592186065087 1061 1679 13194139534654 17592186045417 false] [17592186065087 1061 639753 13194139534654 17592186045417 true] [17592186065088 1061 1681 13194139534654 17592186045417 false] [17592186065088 1061 639756 13194139534654 17592186045417 true] [17592186065435 1064 "Suppose we want to calculate the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, this looks kind of tricky. However, if we use the identity $\\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} = \\csc x,$ we can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}x})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {csc}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we see that this is just a standard trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2x}\\,\\textrm d x  =  \\int\\csc^2 x\\,\\textrm d x =-\\cot x + C\n$$\n\nWhen confronted with a tricky-looking trigonometric integral, we can often reduce it to a standard integral using a trigonometric identity." 13194139534654 17592186045417 false] [17592186065435 1064 "Suppose we want to calculate the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, this looks kind of tricky. However, if we use the identity $\\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} = \\csc x,$ we can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we see that this is just a standard trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2x}\\,\\textrm d x  =  \\int\\csc^2 x\\,\\textrm d x =-\\cot x + C\n$$\n\nWhen confronted with a tricky-looking trigonometric integral, we can often reduce it to a standard integral using a trigonometric identity." 13194139534654 17592186045417 true] [17592186320413 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431090 13194139534654 17592186045417 true] [17592186320413 1078 #edb/ref 17592186319573 13194139534654 17592186045417 false] [17592186320413 1078 #edb/ref 17592186431090 13194139534654 17592186045417 true] [17592186431054 1080 "0" 13194139534654 17592186045417 true] [17592186431054 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534654 17592186045417 true] [17592186431054 1083 #uuid "02b90dc2-4752-56a7-9b8f-d500c9d40dff" 13194139534654 17592186045417 true] [17592186431055 1080 "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{9}" 13194139534654 17592186045417 true] [17592186431055 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534654 17592186045417 true] [17592186431055 1083 